  "modernization": {
    "modern_text": "Dear Sir, Monticello Sep. 30. 1781. The bearer Mr. William Short purposing to Philadelphia for the prosecution of his studies, I take the liberty of introducing him to your notice. To this I am induced not more by my friendship for him, than by a desire of availing myself of the opportunity of assuring you that I cherish as a valuable acquisition the acquaintance with which you were pleased to honour me in Philadelphia, and of adding to the number of your well wishers a gentleman of very uncommon genius, learning, and merit. I have the honor to be with great respect and esteem, Dear Sir, your most humble servant, Th. Jefferson",
    "plain_english": "Dear Sir, writing from Monticello on September 30, 1781, Thomas Jefferson introduces Mr. William Short, who is traveling to Philadelphia to continue his studies. Jefferson explains that he is writing both out of friendship for Short and to thank the recipient for their past acquaintance in Philadelphia, which Jefferson values highly. He also presents Short as a remarkably talented, educated, and worthy gentleman. Jefferson closes by expressing his great respect and esteem as the recipient's humble servant.",
    "summary": "Thomas Jefferson writes to an acquaintance in Philadelphia on September 30, 1781, to introduce a young scholar named William Short. Jefferson praises Short's exceptional talent and character while also expressing his appreciation for his own past relationship with the recipient.",
